+#### Max Consecutive Losses
+
+`MaxConsecutiveLosses` looks at trades for a pair (or, unless `only_per_pair` is set to true, across all pairs) that closed within `lookback_period` in minutes (or in candles when using `lookback_period_candles`), and walks backwards from the most recent trade counting a streak of consecutive losing trades (`close_profit` below `required_profit`).
+
+Unlike `StoplossGuard`, which counts any qualifying trades within the lookback window regardless of order, `MaxConsecutiveLosses` requires the losses to be uninterrupted: a single winning (or breakeven) trade resets the streak to zero.
+
+Once the streak reaches `trade_limit`, trading will stop for `stop_duration` in minutes (or in candles when using `stop_duration_candles`, or until the set time when using `unlock_at`).
+
+This applies across all pairs by default; set `only_per_pair` to true to only lock the specific pair that hit the streak, and never lock trading globally.
+
+For futures bots, setting `only_per_side` will make the bot only consider one side, and will then only lock this one side, allowing for example shorts to continue after a series of long losses.
+
+The below example stops trading for a pair for 4 candles after 3 consecutive losing trades on that pair, without affecting other pairs.
+
+``` python
+@property
+def protections(self):
+    return [
+        {
+            "method": "MaxConsecutiveLosses",
+            "trade_limit": 3,
+            "lookback_period_candles": 24,
+            "stop_duration_candles": 4,
+            "only_per_pair": True,
+        }
+    ]
+```
+
 ### Full example of Protections
 
 All protections can be combined at will, also with different parameters, creating a increasing wall for under-performing pairs.
